                <h2>The Scene: 4.2% CTR, But Negative ROI</h2>
<p>A tool-app advertising team encountered a puzzling problem last week—</p>
<p>They tested 5 new creatives, 2 of which achieved a remarkable 4.2% CTR, far above their account average of 1.8%. The team was thrilled, believing they'd finally cracked the code on viral creatives.</p>
<p>But when they reviewed the numbers on Monday, those 2 "star creatives" delivered only 3 conversion orders, with ROI going negative. Meanwhile, the other 3 creatives with just 1.5% CTR generated 47 orders.</p>
<p>The team was baffled: higher CTR, but no sales?</p>
<hr />
<h2>Problem Breakdown: Your Review Process Might Be Wrong From the Start</h2>
<p>This isn't an isolated case. Many advertising teams default to CTR and CPM as core metrics during creative reviews. But when you confuse "high-performing" (attracting clicks) with "high-converting" (generating revenue), problems arise.</p>
<p><strong>High-CTR creatives typically have these characteristics:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Strong visual impact, headline-grabbing content</li>
<li>Spark curiosity or emotional resonance, but loosely connected to core product benefits</li>
<li>Attract "bystanders" rather than target users</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>High-converting creatives focus on:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Clear product value and purchase motivation</li>
<li>Reaching users with genuine needs</li>
<li>Click-through paths highly aligned with conversion goals</li>
</ul>
<p>Simply put: high-performing creatives make you <em>want to click</em>, while high-converting creatives make you <em>want to buy</em>.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Judgment Method: Three Dimensions to Distinguish Performance from Conversion</h2>
<h3>Dimension 1: Post-Click Behavior Data</h3>
<p>High CTR with poor conversion first requires checking <strong>landing page alignment</strong>. Users click due to ad attraction, but if the landing page doesn't match the ad's promise, drop-off is inevitable.</p>
<p>Key metrics to examine:<br />
- Is page dwell time below 15 seconds?<br />
- Is add-to-cart or registration rate below 1%?<br />
- Is bounce rate above 70%?</p>
<p>If these numbers are poor, the creative is either attracting the wrong audience or there's a gap between promise and delivery.</p>
<h3>Dimension 2: Traffic Quality, Not Volume</h3>
<p>High CTR often means lower CPC (cost per click), but if this traffic isn't targeted, even low CPM is wasteful.</p>
<p>Consider using <strong>Cost Per Acquisition (CPA)</strong> and <strong>first-order profit margin</strong> to measure a creative's true value, rather than CTR or CPC in isolation.</p>
<h3>Dimension 3: Long-Cycle Data, Not Single-Day Spikes</h3>
<p>Some creatives show explosive CTR in the first 3 days but then decay rapidly—these are "novelty creatives" that attract attention through freshness, but users quickly fatigue.</p>
<p>Truly converting creatives typically run stably for 2-3 weeks, with CPA stabilizing or even declining over time.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Action Plan: A Review SOP That Objectively Evaluates Every Creative</h2>
<p>Returning to the opening case: after Yiyumedia's投放顾问 intervened, they helped the team establish a standardized creative review process:</p>
<p><strong>Step 1: Categorize Creative Types</strong></p>
<p>When launching new creatives, explicitly tag them as "traffic drivers" or "profit generators." Traffic drivers accept high CTR with low conversion; profit generators focus on ROI over click rates. Different creatives use different KPIs to avoid blanket evaluation.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Bind UTM Parameters to Trace Full Conversion Paths</strong></p>
<p>Configure independent UTM parameters for each creative to ensure end-to-end data tracking: ad click → landing page → add-to-cart → payment. This reveals exactly which creative is "dragging down" performance.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: 72-Hour Cooling Period to Avoid Premature Judgment</strong></p>
<p>Set a 72-hour observation period for new creatives. During this time, only monitor basic delivery status (normal spending, reasonable bids), without jumping to conclusions. Evaluate CTR, CPM, CPA, and ROI comprehensively after 72 hours.</p>
<p><strong>Step 4: Mandatory A/B Testing</strong></p>
<p>Keep at least 2 creatives from each batch running for comparison, avoiding scattered budget that leads to insufficient sample sizes. Each creative should receive at least 20% of daily account budget to ensure data scale supports decision-making.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Results: From "CTR-First" to "ROI-Driven"</h2>
<p>After implementing the new review process, this team's creative strategy shifted significantly:</p>
